{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 7,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.119,
            0.073,
            0.148,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": "44"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.187,
            0.071,
            0.494,
            0.089
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part II"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.11,
            0.881,
            0.15
          ],
          "content": "\\*3. Divisors occur in pairs. For instance, the divisors of 18 are (1, 18), (2, 9) and (3, 6)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.195,
            0.154,
            0.882,
            0.195
          ],
          "content": "(i) If we write out divisors in pairs, how many numbers do we have to examine between 1 and n to find all the divisors of n?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.189,
            0.199,
            0.882,
            0.24
          ],
          "content": "(ii) If we list out the divisors in pairs, will our gcd algorithm still work in the manner we have described?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.257,
            0.516,
            0.28
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "11.3.1 Improving the Algorithm"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.29,
            0.606,
            0.312
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Compute both lists of divisors together"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.322,
            0.883,
            0.4
          ],
          "content": "In the algorithm above, we first check all numbers between 1 and m to find the divisors of m. Then we start again with 1 and check all numbers between 1 and n to find the divisors of n. This means we have to scan m + n possible divisors."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.404,
            0.881,
            0.445
          ],
          "content": "We can combine these two scans into one. Let max(m, n) be the maximum of m and n. We run through each j from 1 to max(m, n)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.45,
            0.738,
            0.471
          ],
          "content": "• If j divides m we add j to the list of divisors of m."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.476,
            0.725,
            0.497
          ],
          "content": "• If j divides n we add j to the list of divisors of n."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.501,
            0.881,
            0.541
          ],
          "content": "This reduces the number of possible divisors to be checked from m + n to max(m, n)."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.558,
            0.689,
            0.58
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Directly compute the list of common divisors"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.591,
            0.882,
            0.649
          ],
          "content": "If we combine the scans, we can directly compute the list of common divisors, instead of first computing the divisors of each number and then combining the lists."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.144,
            0.655,
            0.881,
            0.695
          ],
          "content": "• For each number j from 1 to max(m, n), if j divides both m and n, add j to the list of common divisors."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.7,
            0.882,
            0.759
          ],
          "content": "In fact, a common divisor cannot be larger than the smaller of the two numbers, so we can check for common divisors up to the minimum of m and n rather than the maximum."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.144,
            0.764,
            0.882,
            0.805
          ],
          "content": "For each number j from 1 to min(m, n), if j divides both m and n, add j to the list of common divisors."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.82,
            0.353,
            0.841
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Do away with lists"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.851,
            0.883,
            0.909
          ],
          "content": "Do we need to maintain the list of all common divisors? Each time we find a larger common divisor, the ones that we have found earlier are useless!"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
